+# Homepage paint monitoring
+
+`performance-monitor.js` passively observes contentful paints caused by interactions
+with navigation links, Order Now, and Seasonal Favorites (including View Details).
+It also observes browser-detected client-side view switches: a trusted interaction
+must cause both a URL change and contentful rendering. It does not add routing.
+
+Inspect `window.dailyGrindPerformance.supported` for browser capabilities and call
+`window.dailyGrindPerformance.getRecords()` for the latest 100 records. Connect an
+analytics consumer without changing the monitor:
+
+```js
+window.addEventListener('daily-grind:performance', ({ detail }) => {
+  console.log(detail);
+});
+```
+
+All durations are milliseconds relative to the initiating interaction. An
+`interaction-paint` record has a control `area`, `label`, `sourcePath`, and `duration`.
+A `view-switch` record has its destination `path`, `fcpDuration`, and `lcpDuration`.
+Only paths and fixed control labels are recorded; no query strings or DOM nodes
+are retained in reports. Records stay local; no analytics endpoint is configured.
+
+Contentful paint entries are progressively larger candidates, including later
+asynchronous updates. Consumers should upsert by `(type, interactionId)` rather
+than count each notification as a separate interaction. View-switch candidates
+are finalized (`final: true`) at the next view switch, page hide, or unload. A
+paint candidate (`final: false`) is not a guarantee that every pixel or animation
+has finished: these APIs measure contentful rendering, not application completion.
+
+The observers are feature-detected and buffered. Unsupported browsers skip
+collection; they do not substitute next-frame or initial-load LCP measurements,
+which cannot measure subsequent asynchronous interaction paints. Event Timing
+IDs attribute controls where available; initiating input timestamps also cover
+fast interactions below Event Timing's reporting threshold.
+
+The current homepage links perform full document navigations. Destination-page
+load timing is outside this document's interaction metrics. View-switch metrics
+become available when a client-side router or view switch produces a browser
+soft-navigation entry; a URL change alone does not qualify.
